# The container nodes

`LoomAst` defines the container nodes, the tree of one loom. The tree comes in two states. The parser builds the first from a file's wefts: a `ParsedDocument` holds one `ParsedSection` per heading or divider, and each section holds a `ParsedHeading` plus its body wefts. A row of small passes then rewrites that tree, as the chapter "Small passes over the tree" describes. The tree the last pass writes is the `LoomDocument`, and the product pass, the weave, and the editor read that tree and no other.

The tree is flat in both states. Heading level is for the reader, never nesting, and a division stands between the sections around it rather than holding them.

## The heading

A `ParsedHeading` is what opens a section. It carries the parts the `HeadingWeft` carried: a `marker`, an optional title, an optional `specifier`, and an optional `sink`. The `specifier` is the heading's braces as the author wrote them, one label or parameters in full, and they name the section's language, its kind, or both. The marker is a heading's run of `#` marks, or a divider's dashes when a divider opens the section. A division's title is its label, the line of prose directly above the divider, so the heading of a division spans both lines. A bare divider gives a heading with no title. A divider carries no specifier and no sink.

=>

export const ParsedHeadingSchema = loomNode('LoomHeading', {
  marker: Schema.Union([HeadingStartTokenSchema, DividerTokenSchema]),
  title: Schema.optional(HeadingTitleTokenSchema),
  specifier: Schema.optional(Schema.Union([SpecifierTokenSchema, ParametersTokenSchema])),
  sink: Schema.optional(SinkTokenSchema),
})
export type ParsedHeading = typeof ParsedHeadingSchema.Type

## The section

A `ParsedSection` is one structural unit, opened by a heading at any level or by a divider. Its body is two ordered runs of wefts: the `preamble` before the first transition, and the `code` after — the `=>` and `~` chunks in source order.

=>

export const ParsedSectionSchema = loomNode('LoomSection', {
  heading: ParsedHeadingSchema,
  preamble: Schema.Array(PreambleWeftSchema),
  code: Schema.Array(SectionBodyWeftSchema),
})
export type ParsedSection = typeof ParsedSectionSchema.Type

## The frontmatter

A `LoomFrontmatter` is a chapter's opening block, parsed. It keeps one shape in both states of the tree. It carries the membership line's `part`, `partName`, `chapter` or `discussion`, and `title`, the `trope` the loom roots or the `rootedIn` it belongs to, the `entries` a trope's root lists, the `book` the loom belongs to, and the block's fields: the `package` a `{Tangle}` section writes to, the `language` its sections inherit, and the `status` and `priority` a roadmap reads. A numbered part fills `part`; a numberless one fills `partName` alone. Every slot is optional, so a partial block still parses to a node the diagnostics can fault, and a file with no block has no frontmatter at all.

A loom whose block names a `discussion` is a discussion rather than a chapter, as the chapter "Discussions" describes. A loom whose block names a `kind` is a loom of a kind some trope declares, and the row of small passes judges the kind and marks it with what the trope says of its looms, as the chapter "Small passes over the tree" describes. A loom whose block names a `trope` is a chapter of that trope, as Part XIII, Tropes, describes. A loom whose block names a `book` and no place in it opens that book, as the chapter "Books" describes.

=>

export const LoomFrontmatterSchema = loomNode('LoomFrontmatter', {
  part: Schema.optional(FrontmatterPartTokenSchema),
  partName: Schema.optional(FrontmatterPartNameTokenSchema),
  chapter: Schema.optional(FrontmatterChapterTokenSchema),
  discussion: Schema.optional(FrontmatterDiscussionTokenSchema),
  kind: Schema.optional(FrontmatterKindTokenSchema),
  trope: Schema.optional(FrontmatterTropeTokenSchema),
  rootedIn: Schema.optional(FrontmatterTropeTokenSchema),
  entries: Schema.optional(Schema.Array(FrontmatterEntryTokenSchema)),
  book: Schema.optional(FrontmatterBookTokenSchema),
  title: Schema.optional(FrontmatterTitleTokenSchema),
  package: Schema.optional(FrontmatterValueTokenSchema),
  language: Schema.optional(FrontmatterValueTokenSchema),
  status: Schema.optional(FrontmatterValueTokenSchema),
  priority: Schema.optional(FrontmatterValueTokenSchema),
})
export type LoomFrontmatter = typeof LoomFrontmatterSchema.Type

## The document

A `ParsedDocument` is the root, the implicit module named by its file. Its `frontmatter` is the opening block, when the file has one; its `preamble` is the run of wefts between that block and the first heading — carrying any value Warps; and its `sections` are the flat list that follows. Every slot may be empty.

=>

export const ParsedDocumentSchema = loomNode('LoomDocument', {
  frontmatter: Schema.optional(LoomFrontmatterSchema),
  preamble: Schema.Array(PreambleWeftSchema),
  sections: Schema.Array(ParsedSectionSchema),
})
export type ParsedDocument = typeof ParsedDocumentSchema.Type

## The tree after a pass

A pass states its work as an edit of the tree before it. Each node the pass changes gets a schema of its own: the earlier node's fields, with the changed fields written over them. The nodes that hold a changed node follow, up to the document.

The pass that expands short forms writes every label of a heading in full. After it, a heading's `specifier` holds parameters only, so no later reader meets a label on a heading. `ExpandedHeadingSchema` is that edit, and `ExpandedSectionSchema` and `ExpandedDocumentSchema` hold it.

=>

export const ExpandedHeadingSchema = Schema.Struct({
  ...ParsedHeadingSchema.fields,
  specifier: Schema.optional(ParametersTokenSchema),
})
export type ExpandedHeading = typeof ExpandedHeadingSchema.Type

export const ExpandedSectionSchema = Schema.Struct({
  ...ParsedSectionSchema.fields,
  heading: ExpandedHeadingSchema,
})
export type ExpandedSection = typeof ExpandedSectionSchema.Type

export const ExpandedDocumentSchema = Schema.Struct({
  ...ParsedDocumentSchema.fields,
  sections: Schema.Array(ExpandedSectionSchema),
})
export type ExpandedDocument = typeof ExpandedDocumentSchema.Type

~

The pass that claims kinds gives a section the kind its heading names, when the core knows that kind. `ClaimedSectionSchema` adds the `kind` field. It holds the kind under the name the core gives it, so a heading that writes `{kind=tangle}` claims `Tangle`. A section whose heading names no kind, or names one the core does not know, has no `kind`. A kind may hold no code, as a concept holds none. A section of such a kind carries `codeless`, and so does every section of a loom whose own kind holds none. The mark is a `Codeless`, which names the kind and says whether it is the section's or the loom's, so a later pass reads the rule from the section without asking which kinds follow it. `ClaimedDocumentSchema` holds the claimed sections.

=>

export const ClaimedSectionSchema = Schema.Struct({
  ...ExpandedSectionSchema.fields,
  kind: Schema.optional(Schema.String),
  codeless: Schema.optional(CodelessSchema),
})
export type ClaimedSection = typeof ClaimedSectionSchema.Type

export const ClaimedDocumentSchema = Schema.Struct({
  ...ExpandedDocumentSchema.fields,
  sections: Schema.Array(ClaimedSectionSchema),
})
export type ClaimedDocument = typeof ClaimedDocumentSchema.Type

## The tree every reader takes

The `LoomDocument` is the tree the last pass of the row writes. Its nodes carry the names every reader after the row uses: `LoomHeading`, `LoomSection`, and `LoomDocument`. The last pass is the one that claims kinds. So a `LoomSection` carries its kind, and its `LoomHeading` is the heading the pass before it wrote. A pass added to the row takes these names for its own tree, so no reader is renamed.

=>

export const LoomHeadingSchema = ExpandedHeadingSchema
export type LoomHeading = ExpandedHeading

export const LoomSectionSchema = ClaimedSectionSchema
export type LoomSection = ClaimedSection

export const LoomDocumentSchema = ClaimedDocumentSchema
export type LoomDocument = ClaimedDocument

## A bearer of braces

Three constructs take braces: a heading, an anchor, and a box. A reader that asks what a pair of braces may hold needs the construct that bears them, as the editor does while an author writes the braces. A `Bearer` is that construct. A heading comes with its section, because the section carries the kind it claimed. An anchor comes with the place it stands, in prose or in code, because a kind of anchor may keep to one of them.

=>

export type Bearer =
  | { readonly on: 'heading'; readonly section: LoomSection }
  | { readonly on: 'anchor'; readonly anchor: WarpAnchorToken; readonly standing: Standing }
  | { readonly on: 'box'; readonly box: BoxToken }

## What this module draws on

Each container is a `loomNode` built from Effect's `Schema`. The heading's subtokens come from `LoomTokens`, with the anchor, the box, and the standing that a bearer of braces holds; the preamble and body weft types come from `Weft`.

=>

import { Schema } from 'effect'
import { loomNode } from '#ast/LoomNode'
import {
  type BoxToken,
  CodelessSchema,
  DividerTokenSchema,
  FrontmatterBookTokenSchema,
  FrontmatterChapterTokenSchema,
  FrontmatterDiscussionTokenSchema,
  FrontmatterKindTokenSchema,
  FrontmatterTropeTokenSchema,
  FrontmatterEntryTokenSchema,
  FrontmatterPartTokenSchema,
  FrontmatterPartNameTokenSchema,
  FrontmatterTitleTokenSchema,
  FrontmatterValueTokenSchema,
  HeadingStartTokenSchema,
  HeadingTitleTokenSchema,
  ParametersTokenSchema,
  SinkTokenSchema,
  SpecifierTokenSchema,
  type Standing,
  type WarpAnchorToken,
} from './LoomTokens'
import { PreambleWeftSchema, SectionBodyWeftSchema } from './Weft'

